00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:29.480
First is artificial intelligence
and machine learning.

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:31.970
And second is Kubernetes.

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:35.660
Both are critical pillars
in today's modern computing

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:38.040
environment.

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:42.510
In today's digital economy,
data is the currency.

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:47.230
For many of us, data is the
lifeblood of our company.

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:51.840
And we want to convert data
into meaningful information,

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.510
actionable intelligence.

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:58.770
AI/ML is the engine that
helps us convert the data

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:01.200
into actionable intelligence.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.670
And with the actionable
intelligence,

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:06.420
we can make better
business decisions

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:09.690
and drive actions
in our organization.

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:13.860
Industries all around the world
are investing very heavily

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:16.800
into AI/ML capabilities.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:22.900
At Google, our mission is
to make AI/ML simple, fast,

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:24.550
and cost-effective.

00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:29.010
We want to make AI/ML simple so
everyone in your organization

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:30.790
can benefit from it.

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:33.030
We want to make
AI/ML fast so you

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:36.900
can build a model fast,
train them fast, deploy them

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:40.330
fast, and get insight faster.

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:43.320
We also want to make
AI/ML cost-effective

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:46.590
in order to democratize
AI/ML so that it's

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:48.390
accessible to everyone.

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:53.320
So when we think about
AI/ML, we typically

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:56.980
think about building
models and training models.

00:02:56.980 --> 00:03:01.720
But in reality, AI/ML involves
a lot more than building

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:02.980
and training models.

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:06.100
Typically the AI/ML code
is a very small part

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:09.070
of the end-to-end
AI/ML tool chain.

00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:13.480
We need to recognize that
AI/ML is a team sport.

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:15.970
A lot of skills
are needed in order

00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:19.400
to build end-to-end
AI/ML application.

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:22.870
For example, a data
engineer may gather the data

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:24.410
and clean the data.

00:03:24.410 --> 00:03:28.165
Another engineer may extract
feature out of the data.

00:03:28.165 --> 00:03:31.190
A data scientist
may build a model.

00:03:31.190 --> 00:03:34.060
An application engineer
may convert the model

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:36.430
into application APIs.

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:42.100
And an end user may use the
model built using those APIs.

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:46.300
So anyone who has ever
tried to build an AI/ML team

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:50.560
knows that there is a
shortage of AI/ML skills.

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:55.220
So how can we make these scarce
resources 10x more productive?

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:57.910
How can we give them
an environment that

00:03:57.910 --> 00:04:01.340
helps them innovate faster?

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:06.890
That's why we have built a
cloud-native AI/ML platform.

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:10.000
So world is moving towards
cloud-native computing.

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:12.970
And Kubernetes is
the de facto standard

00:04:12.970 --> 00:04:15.810
for orchestrating
containerized workload.

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:18.160
We have brought the
power of Kubernetes

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:20.260
to the world of AI/ML.

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:25.240
Kubernetes makes AI/ML
workflows simple, composable,

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:27.040
and scalable.

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:29.125
With the help of
Kubernetes platform,

00:04:29.125 --> 00:04:32.680
a data scientist can
train and deploy a model

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:37.030
without having to worry about
underlying infrastructure.

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:40.060
Simply choose your
favorite AI/ML framework,

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:42.640
pack the model up
into containers,

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:45.960
and deploy them on
Kubernetes at scale.

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:48.040
Kubernetes will
scale your models

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:50.590
from a single node
to thousands of nodes

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:52.870
depending on your
compute requirement.

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:54.500
It will make you
more productive.

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:58.420
And the best part is you will
be able to reproduce results

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:00.355
reliably across the platform.

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:05.940
So let's think about why
Kubernetes is an ideal platform

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:09.180
for building AI/ML tool chain.

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:12.280
So there are three
core reasons why.

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:14.370
First is portability.

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:19.000
Kubernetes enables
cloud-native open standard API

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:21.910
which means you
can seamlessly port

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:25.290
your workload between
cloud, and laptop,

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:28.320
and your own private data
center without having

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:30.490
to change your source code.

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:34.150
Number two, it makes
your workload scalable.

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:39.240
You can scale from a single
node to thousands of nodes.

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:42.720
Also Kubernetes supports
hardware accelerators

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:46.050
such as GPUs and
TPUs and provides you

00:05:46.050 --> 00:05:49.920
high-level abstractions
like auto scaling or auto

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:53.130
provisioning which makes
scaling your workload

00:05:53.130 --> 00:05:55.390
super simple and easy.

00:05:55.390 --> 00:05:58.165
And number three
is productivity.

00:05:58.165 --> 00:06:01.830
With the help of Kubernetes,
you can free up your users

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:05.580
from managing their
own workstations or VM.

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:07.350
As a data scientist
and data engineer,

00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:10.260
you can focus on your
core mission which

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:12.840
is to basically build
and deployment models

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:16.450
without having to worry about
underlying infrastructure.

00:06:16.450 --> 00:06:19.110
So if you are a data
scientist, you are wondering,

00:06:19.110 --> 00:06:21.660
do I have to master the
Kubernetes skill in order

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:24.000
to benefit from
all these features?

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:27.630
Great news, you don't have
to master those skills.

00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:30.030
Data scientists typically
build their model

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:31.740
inside the Notebooks.

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:34.620
They have their preferences
for their libraries and machine

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:36.030
learning frameworks.

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:38.770
To make their work
super simple and easy,

00:06:38.770 --> 00:06:41.670
we are providing
hosted Notebooks.

00:06:41.670 --> 00:06:44.970
And our hosted Notebooks
are pre-configured with one

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:49.080
of the widely-used
libraries like Pandas

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:52.110
and also many other
machine learning

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:56.440
frameworks like TensorFlow,
Keras, and many, many more.

00:06:56.440 --> 00:06:59.310
All you have to do is
open up your browser,

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:02.460
connect to your data,
and start building models

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:03.990
inside your Notebooks.

00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:07.140
The best part is, you
can train the model

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:10.620
and deploy your model
on the top of Kubernetes

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:12.170
directly from your Notebook.

00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:13.920
You don't even have
to leave the Notebook.

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:17.310
So you get very
Notebook-friendly experience

00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:21.270
and all the benefits
of Kubernetes.

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:24.780
So what if you are an
expert Kubernetes user?

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:27.660
Great news, Kubernetes
also provides you

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:32.820
high level abstraction that
helps you run TensorFlow jobs

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:35.020
very simply and easily.

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:38.310
For example, you can
make use of TFJob.

00:07:38.310 --> 00:07:40.830
What the TFJob does
is it integrates

00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:43.260
TensorFlow distributor
training and estimator

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:45.810
APIs with Kubernetes.

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:49.980
And it allows you to scale
training and leverage

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:54.210
the hardware accelerators
which work with Kubernetes.

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:59.430
As a user, you can benefit
from a very vibrant ecosystem

00:07:59.430 --> 00:08:02.100
of Kubernetes products
that are out there when

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:06.090
you work and develop your
models on the top of Kubernetes.

00:08:06.090 --> 00:08:09.120
You can use Kubernetes
dashboards for monitoring

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:12.160
or Prometheus, and Grafana,
and many, many other tools

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:16.890
which community have
developed over many years.

00:08:16.890 --> 00:08:19.610
So Kubernetes also
makes TensorFlow

00:08:19.610 --> 00:08:22.070
serving super simple and easy.

00:08:22.070 --> 00:08:25.250
So let's say you build a
very sophisticated movie

00:08:25.250 --> 00:08:27.080
recommendation engine.

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:31.220
Now you want to test your
model on a small scale

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:33.210
with the global traffic.

00:08:33.210 --> 00:08:35.690
Now, if you deploy
your model in US,

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:38.000
the traffic will
be overwhelmingly

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:41.000
from the audience which
loves Hollywood movie.

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:43.100
If you deploy this
model in India,

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:45.200
the traffic will be
from overwhelmingly

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:47.720
from the audience which
loves Bollywood movie.

00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:49.110
So you have a dilemma.

00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:52.820
On the one hand, you
want to test your model

00:08:52.820 --> 00:08:55.190
through a global
cross-section of the traffic.

00:08:55.190 --> 00:08:57.590
On the other hand,
you don't want

00:08:57.590 --> 00:09:00.140
to deploy your model
globally, which-- because it's

00:09:00.140 --> 00:09:01.400
an experimental model.

00:09:01.400 --> 00:09:03.620
And you are concerned
about the blast radius

00:09:03.620 --> 00:09:05.060
of something going wrong.

00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:06.260
So what do you do?

00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:09.560
Here again, Kubernetes and
Istio you come to the rescue.

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:12.800
With the help of
Kubernetes and Istio

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:16.070
you can actually configure
the your load balancer

00:09:16.070 --> 00:09:18.830
so that your model
gets a cross section

00:09:18.830 --> 00:09:21.120
of the traffic from many,
many different regions.

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:25.070
So you can test the fidelity of
your model across the regions

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:28.560
without having to deploy
your model globally.

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:32.610
So what it means is Kubernetes
helps you address many model

00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:34.040
roll out challenges.

00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:36.950
It makes model lifecycle
management super

00:09:36.950 --> 00:09:39.290
simple and easy.

00:09:39.290 --> 00:09:42.170
So so far, I talked
about how Kubernetes

00:09:42.170 --> 00:09:44.300
simplifies model building.

00:09:44.300 --> 00:09:48.260
But the biggest challenge
is model training.

00:09:48.260 --> 00:09:51.260
We have seen time and time
that building a complex model

00:09:51.260 --> 00:09:54.060
takes really, really long
time, mainly because it takes

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:56.670
a very long time to train them.

00:09:56.670 --> 00:09:59.810
So in order to expedite
the model training,

00:09:59.810 --> 00:10:03.590
we have brought the power
of GPUs to the Kubernetes.

00:10:03.590 --> 00:10:06.520
So with the help of
GPUs, you can turbo

00:10:06.520 --> 00:10:10.580
charge your compute-intensive
applications.

00:10:10.580 --> 00:10:15.470
We support a wide range of GPUs
in Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:18.800
And they have a different cost
versus performance profile,

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:23.480
so you can choose the GPU that
best suits your application

00:10:23.480 --> 00:10:25.610
needs and your budget.

00:10:25.610 --> 00:10:30.380
You can get started with a
Kubernetes GPU K80, which

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:33.590
we support, which gives
you entry-level performance

00:10:33.590 --> 00:10:34.970
at a lower cost.

00:10:34.970 --> 00:10:38.090
But if you decide to turbo
charge your application,

00:10:38.090 --> 00:10:41.930
you can use V100, which
is very powerful GPU

00:10:41.930 --> 00:10:44.720
and allows you to expedite
your model building,

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:47.780
and model training, as
well as model serving.

00:10:47.780 --> 00:10:53.840
And the best part is that GKE
also supports preemptible GPU.

00:10:53.840 --> 00:10:58.640
So for your batch workload,
you can reduce the cost

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:02.615
by 50% to 70% by using
pre-emptible GPUs.

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:09.350
So we also offer you many
more choices for hardware

00:11:09.350 --> 00:11:12.950
accelerators to accelerate
your machine learning workload.

00:11:12.950 --> 00:11:16.370
So for example, on
Google Cloud GKE,

00:11:16.370 --> 00:11:19.820
we also support Google
Design hardware accelerators

00:11:19.820 --> 00:11:23.030
which we call Cloud
TPU, which will further

00:11:23.030 --> 00:11:26.930
accelerate your AI/ML workload
while reducing the cost.

00:11:26.930 --> 00:11:29.240
And I am super
pleased to announce

00:11:29.240 --> 00:11:32.660
that our third generation
Cloud TPUs, version three,

00:11:32.660 --> 00:11:35.750
are now generally available
on Google Kubernetes

00:11:35.750 --> 00:11:38.510
Engine starting today.

00:11:38.510 --> 00:11:42.500
So you can access those
very powerful Cloud TPUs use

00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:47.270
just like you will access
CPUs and GPUs on GKE.

00:11:47.270 --> 00:11:49.660
You don't have to
manage them on your own.

00:11:49.660 --> 00:11:53.480
Google Kubernetes Engine
actually manages them for you.

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:56.510
Also, the best part,
they are available

00:11:56.510 --> 00:12:00.230
as a preemptible load, which
means you can substantially

00:12:00.230 --> 00:12:02.480
reduce the cost
of batch training

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:04.050
by using preemptible Cloud TPUs.

00:12:07.820 --> 00:12:11.420
Google Kubernetes Engine also
supports cluster auto scaler

00:12:11.420 --> 00:12:13.560
with GPU and TPUs.

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:15.620
So with the help of
cluster auto scaler,

00:12:15.620 --> 00:12:19.190
you can automatically scale
up and down the clusters

00:12:19.190 --> 00:12:22.550
for best performance over cost.

00:12:22.550 --> 00:12:26.720
So nodes with GPU and
TPU get created when you

00:12:26.720 --> 00:12:29.430
need extra compute capacity.

00:12:29.430 --> 00:12:32.240
You don't have to
manually schedule them.

00:12:32.240 --> 00:12:36.170
And the nodes with GPU and TPU
get deleted when they are idle.

00:12:36.170 --> 00:12:40.460
So you get two core features,
on demand extra compute

00:12:40.460 --> 00:12:43.520
without you having to
manually scheduled them,

00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:46.940
and number two, freeing up the
resources when they are not

00:12:46.940 --> 00:12:49.560
used, which means you
don't have to pay for them.

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:52.190
So it optimizes the cost and
gives you a super computing

00:12:52.190 --> 00:12:56.380
powers without manual work.

00:12:56.380 --> 00:12:58.870
Now let's look at a
real-world example.

00:12:58.870 --> 00:13:02.460
What would be a better example
than a software program

00:13:02.460 --> 00:13:07.170
that defeated world champions
and professional Go players?

00:13:07.170 --> 00:13:09.840
This was a historical
milestone in the history

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:12.690
of artificial intelligence when
a software program defeated

00:13:12.690 --> 00:13:14.430
world champions.

00:13:14.430 --> 00:13:16.560
So for those who are
familiar with Go,

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:20.190
Go is a strategy board
game which was invented

00:13:20.190 --> 00:13:22.030
more than 2,500 years ago.

00:13:22.030 --> 00:13:26.610
And ever since, it has
captivated the human mind.

00:13:26.610 --> 00:13:29.340
It is played on
a 19-by-19 board.

00:13:29.340 --> 00:13:33.330
And players actually
alternate placing the stones

00:13:33.330 --> 00:13:35.100
on an empty cross-section.

00:13:35.100 --> 00:13:39.540
And the player that surrounds
more territory actually wins.

00:13:39.540 --> 00:13:43.580
So Go is incredibly
difficult program

00:13:43.580 --> 00:13:45.320
for a computer to learn.

00:13:45.320 --> 00:13:47.030
And here is why.

00:13:47.030 --> 00:13:50.390
Just imagine that the number
of board configurations that

00:13:50.390 --> 00:13:53.030
are possible on a Go
board are actually more

00:13:53.030 --> 00:13:56.000
than the number of
atoms in the universe,

00:13:56.000 --> 00:13:58.230
number of atoms in the universe.

00:13:58.230 --> 00:14:01.820
So just a brute force
approach does not work.

00:14:01.820 --> 00:14:06.770
Number two, for every move,
you have too many choices

00:14:06.770 --> 00:14:07.790
available.

00:14:07.790 --> 00:14:11.510
And its impact is realized
over a large horizon which

00:14:11.510 --> 00:14:13.640
makes this very, very
difficult for a computer

00:14:13.640 --> 00:14:15.110
to analyze through.

00:14:15.110 --> 00:14:17.690
And number three,
unlike chess which

00:14:17.690 --> 00:14:20.970
has rules when the game
is over, for example,

00:14:20.970 --> 00:14:22.910
one player checkmates
the other, Go

00:14:22.910 --> 00:14:26.300
has no rule where it dictates
the termination of a game.

00:14:26.300 --> 00:14:28.490
Game is over when
two parties agree,

00:14:28.490 --> 00:14:31.010
which means it's very
difficult for a computer

00:14:31.010 --> 00:14:34.010
to analyze when
the game is over.

00:14:34.010 --> 00:14:37.970
So AlphaGo Zero was
the first program

00:14:37.970 --> 00:14:40.100
which defeated the
professional Go player.

00:14:40.100 --> 00:14:41.930
And I'm going to
talk about Minigo,

00:14:41.930 --> 00:14:45.740
which is an open-source
independent implementation

00:14:45.740 --> 00:14:46.650
of the AlphaGo Zero.

00:14:49.480 --> 00:14:53.120
So let's see how Go
algorithm is implemented.

00:14:53.120 --> 00:14:55.330
So Minigo Gore is
actually implemented using

00:14:55.330 --> 00:14:57.480
reinforced learning algorithm.

00:14:57.480 --> 00:15:02.420
So in reinforced learning,
there are two critical steps.

00:15:02.420 --> 00:15:06.410
In the first step, the
model plays against itself,

00:15:06.410 --> 00:15:08.350
which is called a self-play.

00:15:08.350 --> 00:15:14.890
And it records the search
space for every move.

00:15:14.890 --> 00:15:17.620
In the second
step, the data that

00:15:17.620 --> 00:15:21.700
is gathered in the first step
is used to train the model

00:15:21.700 --> 00:15:23.680
and make the model better.

00:15:23.680 --> 00:15:27.400
Now again, this better
model plays against itself

00:15:27.400 --> 00:15:29.260
to create more data.

00:15:29.260 --> 00:15:32.210
And this more data is used
to create even better models.

00:15:32.210 --> 00:15:33.940
So this loops keeps repeating.

00:15:33.940 --> 00:15:36.850
And model gets better,
and better, and better.

00:15:36.850 --> 00:15:41.230
Now remember, every Go
game has hundreds of moves.

00:15:41.230 --> 00:15:45.100
And for every move, the
model has to do inference

00:15:45.100 --> 00:15:47.650
for thousands of search spaces.

00:15:47.650 --> 00:15:53.050
So the amount of compute
that is needed for inference

00:15:53.050 --> 00:15:56.410
actually dwarfs the amount of
compute needed for training.

00:15:56.410 --> 00:15:58.460
So inference is the
biggest bottleneck.

00:15:58.460 --> 00:16:00.970
You can see in this
picture, self-play arrow

00:16:00.970 --> 00:16:03.040
is much bigger than
the training arrow.

00:16:03.040 --> 00:16:04.970
It's on purpose.

00:16:04.970 --> 00:16:08.140
So how can we
basically make sure

00:16:08.140 --> 00:16:12.200
that this game can be trained
in a short amount of time?

00:16:12.200 --> 00:16:15.160
So the great news here
is that all these games

00:16:15.160 --> 00:16:18.240
can be played independently
in parallel because they

00:16:18.240 --> 00:16:20.660
are completely independent.

00:16:20.660 --> 00:16:24.370
So using that, we
implemented the Minigo

00:16:24.370 --> 00:16:28.660
on the top of GKE,
Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:16:28.660 --> 00:16:33.460
And initial implementation
used to take more than a couple

00:16:33.460 --> 00:16:35.020
of months to train.

00:16:35.020 --> 00:16:37.630
Now imagine you want to
experiment with that,

00:16:37.630 --> 00:16:40.850
you start training, and then it
says, come back in five months

00:16:40.850 --> 00:16:42.100
and the training will be over.

00:16:42.100 --> 00:16:44.500
That would be quite
frustrating, isn't it?

00:16:44.500 --> 00:16:47.410
So with the help
of Cloud TPUs, we

00:16:47.410 --> 00:16:50.080
could scale down
the training time

00:16:50.080 --> 00:16:52.180
on the top of Google
Kubernetes Engine

00:16:52.180 --> 00:16:54.200
to just a couple of days.

00:16:54.200 --> 00:16:57.910
So after we fully
trained it, the model

00:16:57.910 --> 00:17:02.270
used to play millions of
games and every single day.

00:17:02.270 --> 00:17:05.410
Every second, 15 to
13 games can be played

00:17:05.410 --> 00:17:07.240
with this advanced feature.

00:17:07.240 --> 00:17:11.170
We could devote up to
150 petaflops of compute

00:17:11.170 --> 00:17:13.480
to a single problem very easily.

00:17:16.740 --> 00:17:21.089
So Cloud TPUs are incredibly
powerful machines.

00:17:21.089 --> 00:17:24.170
So this is a picture of
Cloud TPU version two.

00:17:24.170 --> 00:17:27.500
And we used it to train a very
well-known model, which is

00:17:27.500 --> 00:17:31.010
ResNet-50, on ImageNet dataset.

00:17:31.010 --> 00:17:34.580
And with one single Cloud
TPU which has eight core,

00:17:34.580 --> 00:17:38.150
we could train the
entire ResNet-50 model

00:17:38.150 --> 00:17:40.430
in under nine hours.

00:17:40.430 --> 00:17:45.680
And the best part was the total
cost was a little under $39.

00:17:45.680 --> 00:17:50.060
And if we use a preemptible TPU,
the cost was almost one third

00:17:50.060 --> 00:17:52.340
to a little under $13.

00:17:52.340 --> 00:17:56.990
So in $13, you have a
supercomputer at your disposal

00:17:56.990 --> 00:17:59.120
to train a very complex model.

00:17:59.120 --> 00:18:01.790
Isn't that incredible?

00:18:01.790 --> 00:18:05.360
And with version three and using
many, many chips in parallel,

00:18:05.360 --> 00:18:08.210
in this case 1,024
chips in parallel,

00:18:08.210 --> 00:18:12.740
we could train a ResNet-50
model on ImageNet dataset up

00:18:12.740 --> 00:18:17.870
to 76.3% accuracy
in just 2.2 minutes.

00:18:17.870 --> 00:18:20.690
It's just blowing to
think that this machine.

00:18:20.690 --> 00:18:25.710
Could process one million
images per second, more than one

00:18:25.710 --> 00:18:26.210
million.

00:18:26.210 --> 00:18:29.750
That's just amazing you could
train models in two minutes

00:18:29.750 --> 00:18:31.880
by the time you
drink your coffee.

00:18:31.880 --> 00:18:33.590
So it will save
you so much time.

00:18:33.590 --> 00:18:35.360
You can do so much
more with your time,

00:18:35.360 --> 00:18:40.310
do something interesting instead
of waiting for model to finish.

00:18:40.310 --> 00:18:44.960
So in order to help you
get started quickly,

00:18:44.960 --> 00:18:49.910
Google provides many open-source
references for Cloud TPUs.

00:18:49.910 --> 00:18:52.010
And with this
open-source references,

00:18:52.010 --> 00:18:54.720
you can solve many
real-life problems.

00:18:54.720 --> 00:18:59.150
For example, you can create
image recognition algorithms.

00:18:59.150 --> 00:19:01.250
Or you can create
machine translation,

00:19:01.250 --> 00:19:04.100
or do speech recognition,
or image relation,

00:19:04.100 --> 00:19:05.930
or many, many different things.

00:19:05.930 --> 00:19:09.020
All you have to do
is bring your data,

00:19:09.020 --> 00:19:11.150
use the existing
model to train them.

00:19:11.150 --> 00:19:13.280
And you're on a great start.

00:19:13.280 --> 00:19:16.460
But if you choose to decide
to work on a special problem,

00:19:16.460 --> 00:19:19.370
you can always write
something from scratch

00:19:19.370 --> 00:19:22.680
using the libraries
that are made available.

00:19:22.680 --> 00:19:25.410
And we support many, many
different frameworks,

00:19:25.410 --> 00:19:29.310
so you can get started with
all the tutorials and knowledge

00:19:29.310 --> 00:19:32.390
that is built in the community
about TensorFlow, Keras,

00:19:32.390 --> 00:19:35.490
and many, many
different frameworks.

00:19:35.490 --> 00:19:39.030
So now let's hear from BHGE
Digital about their experience

00:19:39.030 --> 00:19:43.080
and their journey of building
AI/ML algorithms and models

00:19:43.080 --> 00:19:44.310
on the top of Kubernetes--

00:19:44.310 --> 00:19:44.960
Mahadevan?

00:19:48.570 --> 00:19:50.320
MAHADEVAN BALASUBRAMANIAM:
Thanks, Maulin.

00:19:50.320 --> 00:19:52.260
So in this portion
of the presentation,

00:19:52.260 --> 00:19:54.720
we'll actually go over
our team's experience

00:19:54.720 --> 00:19:56.110
with using GKE.

00:19:56.110 --> 00:19:59.520
In particular, we will share
some of the best practices

00:19:59.520 --> 00:20:02.400
that we followed so that we
can actually leverage all

00:20:02.400 --> 00:20:04.170
the resources we commission.

00:20:04.170 --> 00:20:08.540
And it's actually simple,
fast, and cost-effective.

00:20:08.540 --> 00:20:11.260
So just to give a scale
of the type of problems

00:20:11.260 --> 00:20:15.050
that we as a larger GE face.

00:20:15.050 --> 00:20:17.920
In every two seconds,
a plane with the GE

00:20:17.920 --> 00:20:20.890
takes off somewhere
in the world.

00:20:20.890 --> 00:20:23.560
Nearly 30% of the
world's electricity

00:20:23.560 --> 00:20:25.780
is actually made
by a GE turbine.

00:20:25.780 --> 00:20:28.810
Coming more closer to
home, we are Baker Hughes.

00:20:28.810 --> 00:20:30.400
We are an oil and gas company.

00:20:30.400 --> 00:20:33.340
So this is the world's
first full-stream company

00:20:33.340 --> 00:20:37.510
which actually takes care
of exploring oil, extracting

00:20:37.510 --> 00:20:41.170
oil, transporting
and refining all

00:20:41.170 --> 00:20:44.030
the way through the
entire lifecycle of oil.

00:20:44.030 --> 00:20:47.800
So in order to sort of manage
all these assets properly,

00:20:47.800 --> 00:20:49.990
we have to deal with
a lot of models.

00:20:49.990 --> 00:20:52.300
You're talking of
thousands of models.

00:20:52.300 --> 00:20:54.610
For us, a model
is something which

00:20:54.610 --> 00:20:58.240
links inputs to its outputs.

00:20:58.240 --> 00:21:00.230
So how do you sort of
build these models?

00:21:00.230 --> 00:21:02.290
So in this case, we
leverage the same framework

00:21:02.290 --> 00:21:05.410
that Maulin mentioned, where
we use GKE that is sitting

00:21:05.410 --> 00:21:07.870
on top of the hardware layer.

00:21:07.870 --> 00:21:11.500
And we utilize the framework
that we first kind of started

00:21:11.500 --> 00:21:14.590
off at Baker Hughes called
the Dependent Docker.

00:21:14.590 --> 00:21:17.800
So this helps an
analytics engineer

00:21:17.800 --> 00:21:20.960
to package their
analytics into containers.

00:21:20.960 --> 00:21:23.860
So it gives a sequence
of scripts and a process

00:21:23.860 --> 00:21:28.060
that can help institutionalize
how analytics could be built,

00:21:28.060 --> 00:21:30.650
run, tested, and deployed.

00:21:30.650 --> 00:21:32.800
So all the analytics
would be actually packaged

00:21:32.800 --> 00:21:34.270
as containers.

00:21:34.270 --> 00:21:36.460
And that would actually
feed applications

00:21:36.460 --> 00:21:38.000
that you see at the top.

00:21:38.000 --> 00:21:40.900
So the applications would
range from data management.

00:21:40.900 --> 00:21:42.310
We deal with a
lot of data, which

00:21:42.310 --> 00:21:45.940
we'll talk about soon, also
a lot of other applications

00:21:45.940 --> 00:21:47.920
where we deal with
anomaly detection

00:21:47.920 --> 00:21:51.760
just to find if a system is
operating normal or abnormal,

00:21:51.760 --> 00:21:54.760
is it time to shut it down or
do some preventative action

00:21:54.760 --> 00:21:55.930
over it.

00:21:55.930 --> 00:21:58.120
There are lots of other
concepts of applications

00:21:58.120 --> 00:22:01.000
where we deal with the amount
of remaining useful life

00:22:01.000 --> 00:22:06.520
that a part has, and definitely,
optimizing the operation.

00:22:06.520 --> 00:22:09.820
In order to do this, we need
to have all these applications.

00:22:09.820 --> 00:22:15.820
And now let us look at what is
a cost of a false prediction.

00:22:15.820 --> 00:22:18.580
A false prediction in our
case can actually mean we need

00:22:18.580 --> 00:22:21.520
to shut down a plant, or
probably we need to sort

00:22:21.520 --> 00:22:23.110
of tell a plane cannot take off.

00:22:23.110 --> 00:22:25.190
And it'll be stuck
at the tarmac.

00:22:25.190 --> 00:22:28.695
The results are
pretty cost-sensitive.

00:22:28.695 --> 00:22:30.070
And we are going
to actually lose

00:22:30.070 --> 00:22:31.540
a lot of money in the process.

00:22:31.540 --> 00:22:33.370
Therefore, we strive
a lot to actually get

00:22:33.370 --> 00:22:34.930
very accurate models.

00:22:34.930 --> 00:22:37.000
So how do you build
accurate models?

00:22:37.000 --> 00:22:41.830
We do have, to build, say,
for a model for a small asset,

00:22:41.830 --> 00:22:44.080
we're looking at
around a five or six

00:22:44.080 --> 00:22:46.960
engineers who spend
around a month or two

00:22:46.960 --> 00:22:50.510
to make a model that
is pretty accurate.

00:22:50.510 --> 00:22:54.250
Now, if we were to scale it for
an entire plant or a refinery,

00:22:54.250 --> 00:22:56.440
I'm talking about
thousands of models.

00:22:56.440 --> 00:22:58.210
No way this model can be scaled.

00:22:58.210 --> 00:23:00.790
Or this traditional way
of bringing in more people

00:23:00.790 --> 00:23:05.050
and trying to sort of
build models cannot scale.

00:23:05.050 --> 00:23:07.090
And on the other flip
side, it is not as simple

00:23:07.090 --> 00:23:09.970
as commissioning more
nodes and clicking a button

00:23:09.970 --> 00:23:12.680
and say go for the
models to be created.

00:23:12.680 --> 00:23:15.310
So we need to do something
different and proper

00:23:15.310 --> 00:23:19.690
to use, leverage all the
machines that we commission.

00:23:19.690 --> 00:23:22.780
So what we will highlight in
the subsequent presentations

00:23:22.780 --> 00:23:26.710
are the things, the bottlenecks
that are there in the process,

00:23:26.710 --> 00:23:29.040
and how we overcame
that so that we

00:23:29.040 --> 00:23:32.740
will be able to build
these models at scale

00:23:32.740 --> 00:23:36.210
while still being
cost-effective.

00:23:36.210 --> 00:23:38.480
So let us focus on
the first example

00:23:38.480 --> 00:23:40.980
where we look at the
data wrangling exercise.

00:23:40.980 --> 00:23:43.180
So we deal with a
lot of data here.

00:23:43.180 --> 00:23:48.380
So we take a simple refinery,
we are looking at our own 3,000

00:23:48.380 --> 00:23:49.880
sensors or so.

00:23:49.880 --> 00:23:53.000
That is generating data the
sensors can be generating data

00:23:53.000 --> 00:23:54.540
at different rates.

00:23:54.540 --> 00:23:55.520
It can be in seconds.

00:23:55.520 --> 00:23:58.460
Some can be in minutes,
a few seconds, et cetera.

00:23:58.460 --> 00:24:00.320
So all these data
has to be connected.

00:24:00.320 --> 00:24:02.870
And therefore, for
the 3,000 sensors,

00:24:02.870 --> 00:24:04.910
over a two-year
period, we are talking

00:24:04.910 --> 00:24:06.590
about trillion rows of data.

00:24:06.590 --> 00:24:08.420
This is pure, raw data.

00:24:08.420 --> 00:24:11.960
And this raw data needs
to be sort of converted

00:24:11.960 --> 00:24:16.220
into some workable data that
can be fed into the analytic.

00:24:16.220 --> 00:24:20.640
And so you can also
expect different--

00:24:20.640 --> 00:24:23.380
analytics would need the
data at different rates.

00:24:23.380 --> 00:24:25.800
So you need to prepare
different sets of data

00:24:25.800 --> 00:24:27.930
to feed different analytics.

00:24:27.930 --> 00:24:29.850
And moreover, all the
raw data that we get

00:24:29.850 --> 00:24:31.500
would not be really clean.

00:24:31.500 --> 00:24:34.875
It has to be cleaned
for some missing data

00:24:34.875 --> 00:24:36.750
because somebody forgot
to turn on the switch

00:24:36.750 --> 00:24:40.120
to record the data, or some
wire got tripped over time.

00:24:40.120 --> 00:24:42.330
So we need to go and
clean up the data,

00:24:42.330 --> 00:24:45.630
fill in the missing data so that
it can be prepared in a form

00:24:45.630 --> 00:24:48.270
that it can be used by
the downstream analytics.

00:24:48.270 --> 00:24:51.150
So the working dataset
that we deal with

00:24:51.150 --> 00:24:54.570
can be of the order
of 1.8 trillion rows.

00:24:54.570 --> 00:24:57.190
And therefore, that is a
lot of data to deal with.

00:24:57.190 --> 00:24:59.950
So how do we sort of
handle it at scale?

00:24:59.950 --> 00:25:02.250
So let us look at
the case of the graph

00:25:02.250 --> 00:25:04.350
that I have shown here
to explain the concept.

00:25:04.350 --> 00:25:06.120
So there are two
axes in the graph.

00:25:06.120 --> 00:25:08.940
The vertical axis is
called the capability axis.

00:25:08.940 --> 00:25:12.510
It's actually telling how
fast of a processor you have,

00:25:12.510 --> 00:25:14.610
how good of a CPU you have.

00:25:14.610 --> 00:25:17.790
So the further north or
further up I go along the axis,

00:25:17.790 --> 00:25:20.370
I am sort of going towards
a supercomputer realm.

00:25:20.370 --> 00:25:22.690
And I am sort of
saturating the process.

00:25:22.690 --> 00:25:26.010
What is on the horizontal axis
is called the capacity machine

00:25:26.010 --> 00:25:29.520
where we actually can
increase our capacity

00:25:29.520 --> 00:25:32.340
or keep capacity by
scaling out the machines,

00:25:32.340 --> 00:25:35.130
and commissioning more
resources, and using them.

00:25:35.130 --> 00:25:37.280
In the case of
data wrangling, we

00:25:37.280 --> 00:25:41.340
are going to process it
typically at a tag-wise level,

00:25:41.340 --> 00:25:43.230
meaning each tag can be taken.

00:25:43.230 --> 00:25:45.180
It can be filled in
for the missing data

00:25:45.180 --> 00:25:47.370
or reassembled to go
and get its answer.

00:25:47.370 --> 00:25:49.200
Or we can deal with
a group of tags

00:25:49.200 --> 00:25:52.680
because there are some
things that physics governs.

00:25:52.680 --> 00:25:56.460
For example, a heat exchanger,
the temperature sensor in it

00:25:56.460 --> 00:25:59.132
is going to be in sync with
the ambient temperature that's

00:25:59.132 --> 00:26:00.090
being measured outside.

00:26:00.090 --> 00:26:01.780
I cannot treat them
as independent,

00:26:01.780 --> 00:26:06.000
so they are going to deal with
sensors by itself or a group

00:26:06.000 --> 00:26:09.450
of sensors that we would know
a priori based on the physics.

00:26:09.450 --> 00:26:13.260
In either case, this process
is pretty well-optimized.

00:26:13.260 --> 00:26:16.310
That's why to
process a single tag

00:26:16.310 --> 00:26:18.450
or a per-tag cost,
which I have indicated

00:26:18.450 --> 00:26:22.380
by the circle over there,
is almost further north

00:26:22.380 --> 00:26:24.240
in the vertical axis,
and I've indicated

00:26:24.240 --> 00:26:26.290
by the circle over there.

00:26:26.290 --> 00:26:32.970
Next if I were to get a certain
target performance where

00:26:32.970 --> 00:26:35.100
we say we want to finish
this particular inline

00:26:35.100 --> 00:26:37.950
operation in a few
hours, that positions

00:26:37.950 --> 00:26:41.700
where our target performance
for in-tags has to go.

00:26:41.700 --> 00:26:43.470
In order to do
that, we can come up

00:26:43.470 --> 00:26:45.690
with how many machines
we have to scale out

00:26:45.690 --> 00:26:47.940
to achieve the performance.

00:26:47.940 --> 00:26:49.830
Later on, Murali
will actually show

00:26:49.830 --> 00:26:52.680
a demo of how we were
able to achieve scale up

00:26:52.680 --> 00:26:53.730
with different nodes.

00:26:53.730 --> 00:26:59.350
And he will specify the cost and
the speed tradeoff over there.

00:26:59.350 --> 00:27:02.260
Next we will actually focus
on the deep-learning example.

00:27:02.260 --> 00:27:03.940
Any deep-learning
model will actually

00:27:03.940 --> 00:27:06.828
go through a process of
an architectural search

00:27:06.828 --> 00:27:08.620
so that we can actually
get the best model.

00:27:08.620 --> 00:27:11.830
We are striving 'till the last
mile but get an accurate model,

00:27:11.830 --> 00:27:14.380
because the cost of
a false prediction

00:27:14.380 --> 00:27:17.350
is pretty steep for
the industrial case.

00:27:17.350 --> 00:27:18.520
How is it typically done?

00:27:18.520 --> 00:27:21.760
A subject matter expert
would take and sample

00:27:21.760 --> 00:27:23.050
different regions.

00:27:23.050 --> 00:27:26.140
He would decide based on how
the model is moving around

00:27:26.140 --> 00:27:32.340
how we could tune the number of
layers, what kind of a dropout

00:27:32.340 --> 00:27:36.430
we need to use, and other
activation parameters.

00:27:36.430 --> 00:27:38.370
So in the case of
BHGE, in order,

00:27:38.370 --> 00:27:40.700
if you're going to build
thousands of models,

00:27:40.700 --> 00:27:45.330
we have automated this
process, where we start off

00:27:45.330 --> 00:27:49.620
with sample space that is
indicated by the red crosses

00:27:49.620 --> 00:27:51.210
that you see over there.

00:27:51.210 --> 00:27:54.180
We train the deep-learning
models in the space.

00:27:54.180 --> 00:27:55.350
And we take the results.

00:27:55.350 --> 00:27:58.080
And we actually are going
to look at certain outcomes

00:27:58.080 --> 00:28:00.090
from those models
with which we actually

00:28:00.090 --> 00:28:03.150
use to fit the model with
those being the outputs,

00:28:03.150 --> 00:28:05.880
and the inputs being
those red crosses.

00:28:05.880 --> 00:28:07.860
That's a meta
model that we built

00:28:07.860 --> 00:28:09.660
which can be very
quickly queried

00:28:09.660 --> 00:28:12.570
for other architectures
that are derived from that.

00:28:12.570 --> 00:28:15.120
We go and search the space
of the meta model, which

00:28:15.120 --> 00:28:17.520
is a surrogate
model, and come up

00:28:17.520 --> 00:28:19.890
with the blue set of
dots which is actually

00:28:19.890 --> 00:28:23.640
[INAUDIBLE] that
indicates the best choices

00:28:23.640 --> 00:28:25.770
for the next pass of runs.

00:28:25.770 --> 00:28:28.380
So from that blue
curve, we can pick

00:28:28.380 --> 00:28:32.235
a couple of dots and
re-learn the process, couple

00:28:32.235 --> 00:28:36.287
it along with the original
red x's to refine the model,

00:28:36.287 --> 00:28:37.745
and keep on continuing
the process.

00:28:37.745 --> 00:28:41.320
So as you can see, this process
is an iterative process.

00:28:41.320 --> 00:28:42.867
You start off with the red x's.

00:28:42.867 --> 00:28:44.700
You come up with your
best candidates, which

00:28:44.700 --> 00:28:46.530
are sitting in your blue curve.

00:28:46.530 --> 00:28:48.210
You then again refine the model.

00:28:48.210 --> 00:28:49.800
You go again to the red curve.

00:28:49.800 --> 00:28:51.540
And you keep on pushing
the process 'till

00:28:51.540 --> 00:28:54.600
then, 'till the point
where you're sort of happy

00:28:54.600 --> 00:28:57.120
or you have enough
tradeoff to the cost

00:28:57.120 --> 00:28:59.830
versus the amount of effort
you're able to put in.

00:28:59.830 --> 00:29:01.943
So in this case,
what you see here,

00:29:01.943 --> 00:29:03.360
we looked at a
deep-learning model

00:29:03.360 --> 00:29:05.790
which had around a 10-plus
architecture parameters

00:29:05.790 --> 00:29:07.260
that we had to tune.

00:29:07.260 --> 00:29:11.100
We actually searched over a
quarter-million architectures.

00:29:11.100 --> 00:29:14.370
And the proof of the pudding is
actually in the results we get.

00:29:14.370 --> 00:29:17.370
So the final answer that
we got out of this process,

00:29:17.370 --> 00:29:20.790
we actually had a 10x
improvement in the training

00:29:20.790 --> 00:29:24.750
effectiveness over a model that
was done by a random search

00:29:24.750 --> 00:29:27.770
by a subject matter expert.

00:29:27.770 --> 00:29:31.310
Next we'll look at how we
dealt with some of the process

00:29:31.310 --> 00:29:33.590
bottlenecks and
how we like removed

00:29:33.590 --> 00:29:37.260
them to get the full speed
that we really need to achieve.

00:29:37.260 --> 00:29:39.680
So first we'll start
looking at the containers.

00:29:39.680 --> 00:29:41.480
As we said, we build
a lot of containers.

00:29:41.480 --> 00:29:43.910
And that's the box that
we show over there.

00:29:43.910 --> 00:29:47.397
And our containers
are encapsulating

00:29:47.397 --> 00:29:48.230
different analytics.

00:29:48.230 --> 00:29:49.940
So it can be with
different complexity.

00:29:49.940 --> 00:29:53.000
So some containers
might need GPUs,

00:29:53.000 --> 00:29:57.200
might utilize them only for
a small level, say, 20%, 30%.

00:29:57.200 --> 00:30:00.620
Others might saturate
it completely.

00:30:00.620 --> 00:30:02.640
So therefore, we need--

00:30:02.640 --> 00:30:05.970
or the containers can actually
have much bigger footprint.

00:30:05.970 --> 00:30:08.210
So if I were to restart
the container every time

00:30:08.210 --> 00:30:11.180
I'm going to make a run, I'm
going to incur a big startup

00:30:11.180 --> 00:30:12.320
time for the container.

00:30:12.320 --> 00:30:15.080
If that is a bottleneck
in your building process,

00:30:15.080 --> 00:30:17.510
we could overcome that
by starting the container

00:30:17.510 --> 00:30:20.510
as a service, where
we can actually

00:30:20.510 --> 00:30:24.020
run an infinite loop to
maintain the container active.

00:30:24.020 --> 00:30:27.190
We can exit into the container,
and sort of run the process,

00:30:27.190 --> 00:30:28.190
and get the answer back.

00:30:28.190 --> 00:30:30.770
So that's how we can
overcome situations

00:30:30.770 --> 00:30:33.290
where the containers
actually have

00:30:33.290 --> 00:30:35.210
a much larger start-up time.

00:30:35.210 --> 00:30:39.290
The second, we need to move
data into the container

00:30:39.290 --> 00:30:41.570
and outside the container
to build models.

00:30:41.570 --> 00:30:44.300
Typically the training
data is pretty big.

00:30:44.300 --> 00:30:46.527
So we don't want
to copy information

00:30:46.527 --> 00:30:47.360
into the containers.

00:30:47.360 --> 00:30:49.670
The way in which we do
it is via map drive.

00:30:49.670 --> 00:30:52.610
So we are avoid copying huge
files into the container

00:30:52.610 --> 00:30:53.780
and bringing it out.

00:30:53.780 --> 00:30:57.230
And all the input and output
that, as I said before,

00:30:57.230 --> 00:31:00.440
are pretty well-structured--
so the output we deal with

00:31:00.440 --> 00:31:02.480
are the outcomes that
we want from the model.

00:31:02.480 --> 00:31:04.910
Those are all
exposed as JSON files

00:31:04.910 --> 00:31:08.160
that we'll be able to
track and move it out.

00:31:08.160 --> 00:31:09.930
So that's how we
handle the data.

00:31:09.930 --> 00:31:12.500
And finally, more
importantly, as I said,

00:31:12.500 --> 00:31:16.400
each container is not going
to be using all the resources

00:31:16.400 --> 00:31:18.540
to its fullest potential.

00:31:18.540 --> 00:31:20.660
I should not assign
this continue

00:31:20.660 --> 00:31:22.490
to run on this particular GPU.

00:31:22.490 --> 00:31:24.200
So what we do here
in this case is,

00:31:24.200 --> 00:31:28.400
we actually run a max
case of the container

00:31:28.400 --> 00:31:31.340
so that we can determine
how much GPU it utilizes

00:31:31.340 --> 00:31:33.470
and how much memory it utilizes.

00:31:33.470 --> 00:31:35.930
Knowing that, we'll be
able to use the fact

00:31:35.930 --> 00:31:39.440
and schedule multiple containers
to actually run on the same GPU

00:31:39.440 --> 00:31:43.640
so that we'll be able to max
out the GPU that we commission.

00:31:43.640 --> 00:31:45.200
So if you sort of
look at the graph,

00:31:45.200 --> 00:31:47.390
I have just intentionally
shown the blue dot

00:31:47.390 --> 00:31:51.290
to be on the lower left just
to indicate conceptually

00:31:51.290 --> 00:31:56.570
that this analytic does not use
the GPU all by itself fully.

00:31:56.570 --> 00:32:00.260
So how do you sort of go up
further north and to the east?

00:32:00.260 --> 00:32:03.290
We can actually use the
fractional resource utilization

00:32:03.290 --> 00:32:06.560
to go up north and use
more number of GPUs

00:32:06.560 --> 00:32:10.335
to go further towards the
right and go towards the star.

00:32:10.335 --> 00:32:11.960
So that is how we'll
be able to achieve

00:32:11.960 --> 00:32:13.970
the best performance
with all the resources

00:32:13.970 --> 00:32:15.710
that we commission.

00:32:15.710 --> 00:32:19.230
Next we will look at the aspect
of orchestrating containers.

00:32:19.230 --> 00:32:22.940
So the entire process was
pretty iterative, as I said.

00:32:22.940 --> 00:32:24.860
And so the way in
which it starts

00:32:24.860 --> 00:32:27.080
is we have a node
which will decide

00:32:27.080 --> 00:32:29.670
what are the next
iterations I need to spawn.

00:32:29.670 --> 00:32:33.080
So it spawns the next set of
iterations, after which it

00:32:33.080 --> 00:32:35.210
actually has to
collate the results

00:32:35.210 --> 00:32:37.520
and decide if I have to
continue with the next set

00:32:37.520 --> 00:32:38.900
of iterations.

00:32:38.900 --> 00:32:42.590
And those intermediate levels
are actually made stateful

00:32:42.590 --> 00:32:45.080
so that I can make a
decision whether I need

00:32:45.080 --> 00:32:48.448
to continue further or not.

00:32:48.448 --> 00:32:49.990
And one of the things
that we noticed

00:32:49.990 --> 00:32:51.910
was, like, there is
a limitation of a

00:32:51.910 --> 00:32:54.340
for loop inside the Kubeflow.

00:32:54.340 --> 00:32:56.950
So we had to
actually unravel it.

00:32:56.950 --> 00:33:01.190
And we made it in a form
where we spawn, we accumulate.

00:33:01.190 --> 00:33:02.320
We again spawn.

00:33:02.320 --> 00:33:05.320
We accumulate the
process to continue it

00:33:05.320 --> 00:33:07.100
and get the results.

00:33:07.100 --> 00:33:11.200
So in order to sort of
reach the full potential

00:33:11.200 --> 00:33:13.510
of all the resources
we commission,

00:33:13.510 --> 00:33:16.600
we had to use this
fractional GPU allocation

00:33:16.600 --> 00:33:19.720
so that we can get a near linear
scale up for the resources

00:33:19.720 --> 00:33:20.262
that we have.

00:33:20.262 --> 00:33:22.012
I will quickly show
the results at the end

00:33:22.012 --> 00:33:23.360
after Murali finishes his talk.

00:33:23.360 --> 00:33:23.860
Thanks.

00:33:26.592 --> 00:33:28.300
MURALI KARUMURI: Yeah,
thanks, Mahadevan.

00:33:32.150 --> 00:33:35.830
As Mahadevan said, we are
an oil and gas industry.

00:33:35.830 --> 00:33:38.460
And we are not a
digital native industry.

00:33:38.460 --> 00:33:41.280
There is a vast difference
between digital native industry

00:33:41.280 --> 00:33:43.860
and an oil and gas industry.

00:33:43.860 --> 00:33:47.280
Usually the data we get
from digital native industry

00:33:47.280 --> 00:33:48.480
is rich.

00:33:48.480 --> 00:33:52.170
And it is almost ready
to be consumable.

00:33:52.170 --> 00:33:54.630
But in oil and gas
industry, we collect

00:33:54.630 --> 00:33:57.150
the data over last 30 years.

00:33:57.150 --> 00:34:00.270
And it can be very messy.

00:34:00.270 --> 00:34:05.100
And very often, we don't even
know what we are looking for,

00:34:05.100 --> 00:34:06.520
unless we explore.

00:34:06.520 --> 00:34:09.270
So the exploration
stage of the data

00:34:09.270 --> 00:34:13.620
itself is quite different from
the digital native industries.

00:34:13.620 --> 00:34:15.690
And it is highly iterative.

00:34:15.690 --> 00:34:19.960
And we have to iterate so
many times 'till the data

00:34:19.960 --> 00:34:21.900
scientists come back
and say that, yes, now

00:34:21.900 --> 00:34:23.840
the data is consumable.

00:34:23.840 --> 00:34:27.510
So I mean, some of you
maybe like me, maybe

00:34:27.510 --> 00:34:30.210
from a traditional
data background,

00:34:30.210 --> 00:34:34.690
and scale up was the solution.

00:34:34.690 --> 00:34:36.909
But now we have scale out.

00:34:36.909 --> 00:34:43.310
And Kubernetes really comes
as a great rescuer here.

00:34:43.310 --> 00:34:48.889
OK, so here I would
like to show--

00:34:48.889 --> 00:34:52.219
I mean, so for the last couple
of years or more than that,

00:34:52.219 --> 00:34:55.639
we have been a very
heavy Kubernetes shop.

00:34:55.639 --> 00:34:59.660
And we use Kubernetes
extensively, both on prem

00:34:59.660 --> 00:35:01.460
and on various cloud platforms.

00:35:01.460 --> 00:35:04.530
And we have a
heterogeneous environment.

00:35:04.530 --> 00:35:08.540
So we have been managing this
Kubernetes clusters on prem.

00:35:08.540 --> 00:35:10.590
And some of the
challenges-- and some of you

00:35:10.590 --> 00:35:14.720
know that the batch management
is the biggest hassle.

00:35:14.720 --> 00:35:16.890
And we need to take downtime.

00:35:16.890 --> 00:35:19.190
And we need to go back
to the data scientists.

00:35:19.190 --> 00:35:22.010
And Mahadevan will
be very frustrated

00:35:22.010 --> 00:35:24.050
that we bring
these clusters down

00:35:24.050 --> 00:35:29.870
in the middle of a big
