00:36:45.590 --> 00:36:49.370
The third aspect is cost.

00:36:49.370 --> 00:36:53.040
Because we provision them
whenever it's needed,

00:36:53.040 --> 00:36:56.320
we don't spend as much money
as we used to do before.

00:36:59.710 --> 00:37:04.330
I will have a simple demo here.

00:37:04.330 --> 00:37:08.930
Before we go to the demo,
let me explain here.

00:37:08.930 --> 00:37:11.740
So we took a simple
query, one of the simplest

00:37:11.740 --> 00:37:14.830
of simple queries as soon
as we received the data.

00:37:14.830 --> 00:37:17.320
That's about 55 billion rows.

00:37:17.320 --> 00:37:21.280
And when we ran
it with 10 nodes,

00:37:21.280 --> 00:37:24.370
it was costing
typically, like, $1.

00:37:24.370 --> 00:37:26.880
And when we scale
it up to 40, nodes

00:37:26.880 --> 00:37:32.080
we finished the job in 130
seconds as against 700 seconds.

00:37:32.080 --> 00:37:36.320
And the cost came down by 28%.

00:37:36.320 --> 00:37:40.010
So this is where we
become a differentiator,

00:37:40.010 --> 00:37:43.520
because we finished the
job in far less time.

00:37:43.520 --> 00:37:46.280
And we achieve it at
far less cost as well.

00:37:52.890 --> 00:37:55.240
So this is the--

00:37:55.240 --> 00:37:59.430
I hope the demo
gods will help me.

00:37:59.430 --> 00:38:03.353
Right, so as you
can see, this is

00:38:03.353 --> 00:38:04.645
a cluster of [INAUDIBLE] nodes.

00:38:07.790 --> 00:38:10.840
And this is the
running average, one

00:38:10.840 --> 00:38:12.650
of the simplest
of simple queries

00:38:12.650 --> 00:38:14.920
that we do as soon as
we receive the raw data

00:38:14.920 --> 00:38:17.600
from the customers.

00:38:17.600 --> 00:38:20.180
So we can see the
progress of the query.

00:38:20.180 --> 00:38:23.560
It's against 40 nodes.

00:38:23.560 --> 00:38:27.590
Sorry, yeah, I
just refreshed it.

00:38:27.590 --> 00:38:33.700
And let me forward it
in the interest of time.

00:38:48.390 --> 00:38:52.070
So as you can see here,
it's 55 billion rows.

00:38:52.070 --> 00:38:54.680
55 billion rows, we
could complete it

00:38:54.680 --> 00:38:57.530
in two minutes and nine seconds.

00:38:57.530 --> 00:39:01.420
And the cost of the
query is less than $1.

00:39:01.420 --> 00:39:02.980
That's what we achieved.

00:39:02.980 --> 00:39:06.240
And this is against
55 billion rows.

00:39:06.240 --> 00:39:11.440
And as Mahadevan said, typically
for a simple equipment,

00:39:11.440 --> 00:39:12.880
what it would make, it--

00:39:12.880 --> 00:39:17.810
when we expand it, it goes
to 1.8 trillion records.

00:39:17.810 --> 00:39:21.700
So we experiment this with
the less number of nodes.

00:39:21.700 --> 00:39:25.340
And then as the need
arises, we scale up.

00:39:25.340 --> 00:39:31.350
And that's how we achieve
at the cheaper rate.

00:39:31.350 --> 00:39:32.306
Thanks, Mahadevan.

00:39:38.903 --> 00:39:40.820
MAHADEVAN BALASUBRAMANIAM:
OK, so I'll quickly

00:39:40.820 --> 00:39:43.130
summarize the results from
the auto scaling experiment

00:39:43.130 --> 00:39:44.310
that we did.

00:39:44.310 --> 00:39:45.793
So where we actually take--

00:39:45.793 --> 00:39:47.210
look at the graph
over here, where

00:39:47.210 --> 00:39:50.240
we are trying to increase
the number of GPUs.

00:39:50.240 --> 00:39:53.060
And we can sort of see
whether we're able to leverage

00:39:53.060 --> 00:39:55.490
and utilize it
completely at all.

00:39:55.490 --> 00:39:56.970
So look at the
bottom two graphs.

00:39:56.970 --> 00:40:00.080
So what you see on the right
side is the number of GPU nodes

00:40:00.080 --> 00:40:02.010
where we are doing
an experiment where

00:40:02.010 --> 00:40:03.530
we are able to
actually increase,

00:40:03.530 --> 00:40:05.720
or in a preset manner,
the number of GPU

00:40:05.720 --> 00:40:09.133
goes from one, to two, to
three, all the way 'till eight.

00:40:09.133 --> 00:40:10.550
And what you see
on the left side,

00:40:10.550 --> 00:40:12.900
the lower left is
actually telling how

00:40:12.900 --> 00:40:15.320
we are able to utilize the GPU.

00:40:15.320 --> 00:40:17.840
So we start off with
one GPU beginning.

00:40:17.840 --> 00:40:19.700
And then we ramp
it from one to two.

00:40:19.700 --> 00:40:22.700
You can immediately see the
percentage utilization drops

00:40:22.700 --> 00:40:23.690
to 50.

00:40:23.690 --> 00:40:26.390
And it again kicks up and
almost goes up instantaneously

00:40:26.390 --> 00:40:27.860
to 100%.

00:40:27.860 --> 00:40:29.830
The next step, again,
go from two to three,

00:40:29.830 --> 00:40:33.170
you can sort of see it ducking
down and again goes up 100%.

00:40:33.170 --> 00:40:34.820
So the main takeaway
from here is

00:40:34.820 --> 00:40:39.110
the way in which we did
the fractional utilization

00:40:39.110 --> 00:40:41.210
and were able to
[INAUDIBLE] to the GPUs,

00:40:41.210 --> 00:40:45.560
we were able to nearly
assign full utilization as we

00:40:45.560 --> 00:40:47.750
increase the number of GPUs.

00:40:47.750 --> 00:40:50.250
And more importantly, as
we take the GPUs away,

00:40:50.250 --> 00:40:51.930
we are also able
to ramp it down.

00:40:51.930 --> 00:40:53.720
So this one just
summarizes how we're

00:40:53.720 --> 00:40:56.240
able to show auto scaling
works when we ramp up

00:40:56.240 --> 00:40:59.350
and ramp down the GPUs.

00:40:59.350 --> 00:41:04.180
The next case is how we actually
make it work on the GKE.

00:41:04.180 --> 00:41:05.920
So in this experiment,
what we did was,

00:41:05.920 --> 00:41:08.650
we had a 32-node cluster.

00:41:08.650 --> 00:41:11.770
And instead of actually
allowing the cluster

00:41:11.770 --> 00:41:14.890
to operate or go up to
100%, we preset the limit

00:41:14.890 --> 00:41:17.060
that it reaches, so to 80%.

00:41:17.060 --> 00:41:19.300
So once we load up the
machine, as was sort of shown

00:41:19.300 --> 00:41:22.930
by the experiment one, and
because we are capping it

00:41:22.930 --> 00:41:25.870
off at 80%, the number
of node requirement

00:41:25.870 --> 00:41:30.250
actually jumps instantaneously
from 32 to all the way to 40.

00:41:30.250 --> 00:41:31.540
It's pretty much flat.

00:41:31.540 --> 00:41:35.410
And once the load tapers
down, it leaves us the GPUs

00:41:35.410 --> 00:41:36.730
and comes back again.

00:41:36.730 --> 00:41:39.040
Just to see the
repeatability, we again

00:41:39.040 --> 00:41:40.120
fired the process again.

00:41:40.120 --> 00:41:41.620
So that is the
experiment two, where

00:41:41.620 --> 00:41:45.210
we fired the same process
with the same constraints, 80%

00:41:45.210 --> 00:41:46.490
as your threshold.

00:41:46.490 --> 00:41:49.020
And as you can
see, the GPU nodes

00:41:49.020 --> 00:41:51.550
that got commissioned
automatically actually

00:41:51.550 --> 00:41:52.390
increased to 40.

00:41:52.390 --> 00:41:53.710
And then it came down.

00:41:53.710 --> 00:41:55.240
For the third
experiment what we did

00:41:55.240 --> 00:41:59.220
was, we wanted to simulate a
case where we partially permit

00:41:59.220 --> 00:42:02.720
an increase, and then
due to some other case,

00:42:02.720 --> 00:42:05.050
we're actually taking the
GPUs way from the system too.

00:42:05.050 --> 00:42:08.800
So we capped it off initially
to some intermediate number

00:42:08.800 --> 00:42:11.620
and then slowly started removing
the GPUs from the system

00:42:11.620 --> 00:42:13.240
to see how it responds.

00:42:13.240 --> 00:42:15.880
As you can see, it
got capped over there.

00:42:15.880 --> 00:42:18.580
And we brought down the
GPUs in a preset sequence

00:42:18.580 --> 00:42:20.020
in the bottom right.

00:42:20.020 --> 00:42:22.180
As you can see at
the top, the time

00:42:22.180 --> 00:42:24.820
it took to sort of complete
the entire process actually

00:42:24.820 --> 00:42:26.960
expanded along the horizontal.

00:42:26.960 --> 00:42:30.730
So again, in order to maintain
a high GPU utilization,

00:42:30.730 --> 00:42:33.360
we need to have a good
way of orchestrating

00:42:33.360 --> 00:42:36.220
and need to design the
underlying containers

00:42:36.220 --> 00:42:37.960
to respect that,
and so that you will

00:42:37.960 --> 00:42:40.410
be able to leverage it fully.

00:42:40.410 --> 00:42:44.580
So in summary, if I were toward
summarize the best practices,

00:42:44.580 --> 00:42:46.770
the cloud scalability
actually requires

00:42:46.770 --> 00:42:49.870
a lot of planning on how
you build the containers,

00:42:49.870 --> 00:42:51.720
how you understand
how much resources

00:42:51.720 --> 00:42:53.520
it needs and manage it.

00:42:53.520 --> 00:42:57.300
And definitely look for a lot
of redesign and iterative steps

00:42:57.300 --> 00:42:58.990
that needs to happen.

00:42:58.990 --> 00:43:02.460
And finally, for load balancing,
the fractional GPU utilization

00:43:02.460 --> 00:43:04.740
and other things has to
be adhered to so that they

00:43:04.740 --> 00:43:06.950
can use the resources fully.

00:43:06.950 --> 00:43:09.410
MURALI KARUMURI:
Thanks, Mahadevan.

00:43:09.410 --> 00:43:11.890
I'd like to say a
couple of things here.

00:43:11.890 --> 00:43:14.490
Scaling up is not finished yet.

00:43:14.490 --> 00:43:15.710
I mean, it can be--

00:43:15.710 --> 00:43:18.530
sometimes it will end
up as a brute force.

00:43:18.530 --> 00:43:21.260
And it is highly iterative.

00:43:21.260 --> 00:43:24.000
A lot of patience and
planning is required.

00:43:24.000 --> 00:43:26.570
And load balancing
is another aspect.

00:43:26.570 --> 00:43:29.330
You've got to think
about it very carefully.

00:43:29.330 --> 00:43:35.090
And adaptive space, be prepared
to have some space so that you

00:43:35.090 --> 00:43:41.210
can redesign your architecture
and take a different route,

00:43:41.210 --> 00:43:45.980
a totally different route
than from where you started.

00:43:45.980 --> 00:43:48.350
It's not easy.

00:43:48.350 --> 00:43:50.710
I mean, now that
you have Kubernetes,

00:43:50.710 --> 00:43:57.290
and particularly, GKE, it's
made it much more simpler.

00:43:57.290 --> 00:44:02.510
Now for example, scaling up
is much more simple right now.

00:44:02.510 --> 00:44:06.680
For example, a typical
scaling up about 200 nodes,

00:44:06.680 --> 00:44:08.600
it happens in less
than a minute.

00:44:11.880 --> 00:44:16.510
So you've got to have a lot of
space to adapt to the changes.

00:44:16.510 --> 00:44:19.720
But you can do a lot of
things with Kubernetes, yeah.

00:44:22.510 --> 00:44:24.520
MAULIN PATEL: Thank you, Murali.

00:44:24.520 --> 00:44:26.370
Thank you Mahadevan
and Murali for sharing

00:44:26.370 --> 00:44:29.470
your insightful
experiences with us today.

